Z||000007||87GTZ34||04-16-2001||10:55 PM||||Hey MOO, what parts have you replaced already aside from the dist to coil connector?  Is the coil working/new, the module etc.  Silly question but did you remove the ADLC diag jumper? Also, 'wont stay running and has no power' could be a sign of a vacuum leak.  I found the diaphram in my EGR had rips in it so instead of the vacuum opening the EGR, I had a 2nd intake.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||jch82||04-16-2001||11:39 PM||||We have put a new coil, cap & rotor, pickup coil, and plugs and wires in so far.....<P>Ed Parks said something about the ADLC diag jumper but we didn't exactly know what that was (sorta mechanically inclined)....<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Yeah, my girlfriend takes collect calls from the road<BR>And it doesn't seem to matter that I'm lacking in the bulge<BR>She laughs at my dumb jokes when no one does<BR>She brings me mexican food from Sombrero's just because<BR>                 <I>-Blink 182</I>||||reg||1
Z||000009||MooCow||04-16-2001||11:57 PM||||This is now what Ed says it is:<BR>Shunting or grounding the ALDL is necessary to correctly set the timing.<BR>Also requires a timing light.<P>However, you can loosen the dist hold down clamp, rotate the dist<BR>CLOCKWISE, until the engine runs good. If you go too far, you will hear<BR>little "rattles" when accelerating. Then turn the distributor a little<BR>counter-clockwise until the noises disappear. That will be the best<BR>timing. No equipment necessary.<P>BTC=before top dead-center. Refers to marks you can see on the timing<BR>cover with a timing light.<P>Sounds like the engine is WAY out of time.<BR>So we are now going to try that and see what happens.  If not I will try what everyone on here says to do.<P>                                Thanks Ken<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||04-17-2001||12:10 AM||||ALDL is located under the panel where the cig lighter is. You just put a wire or paper clip in pins 1 and 2. Top left pins when facing it. You can borrow a timing light from Trak, NAPA, or Giant Auto. Place jumper in start car, The timing mark on the crank pulley is a line. You'll see a sawtooth plate above the pulley. The deep cut is "0*"<BR>Each indent is 2* So you want the line on the crank pulley lined up at the 5th indent. (10* BTDC)<P>The "rattling" you'll hear if you advance too far is called "pinging" or "knock".<P>||||reg||1
